{
  "title": "A coherent 552.16 microHz model nearly matches the 19.48-hour KIC 10001893 fit in corrected Kepler flux",
  "abstract": "We test whether the known 19.48-hour KIC 10001893 signal can be a sampling alias of a faster coherent oscillation. We use 51,679 exposures from 14 Kepler release 25 quarters. We compare five frequency bands with common local trends and free sine coefficients. A first-order model near 552.16 microHz nearly matches the slow fit in corrected flux. After local frequency refinement, slow-model fit gains are only 0.68 and 4.83 ppm squared for 3-day and 6-day trends. Aperture flux gives larger gains of 54.54 and 78.82 ppm squared. Conditional residual-block trials do not give a common strong rejection of the first-order alias in both products. The products share photons. This result leaves the physical source open. It does not detect a fast mode or confirm a planet. The test assumes one coherent sinusoid and independent symmetric 6-day residual blocks. We provide original code, aggregate fits, a figure, input hashes and external retrieval steps.",
